  "narrative": {
    "top_frame": "Two walk-offs in one day. That's not baseball anymore. That's a breakdown.",
    "what_this_means": "Jung Hoo Lee's go-ahead single in the ninth of Game 2 looked like the moment that finally went their way. It didn't. Both games ended the same way: Giants leading late, Giants losing anyway. The lineup worked. They had thirteen hits across the night game alone. Drew Gilbert went 3-for-5. Luis Arraez drove in two. None of it held. Houser gave up three runs in 4.2 innings, and even that damage felt manageable until the ninth inning showed up twice.",
    "what_to_watch": "This is what inconsistency looks like when it tightens. The offense isn't broken. The margin between producing and losing is. They scored five runs in Game 2 and lost. They left runners on base and had the lead and couldn't hold either one. This team doesn't fold on command; it folds when the game is already in their hands. That's different. That's worse. Two chances to close it out, two different scenarios, same result. The pattern isn't new. It's becoming harder to ignore.",
